## What upstream did with ours

**The serial-ring stall we reported at U63 is fixed at its cause**
(COMPILER-104, reek, main 29170). The serial reader polls the UART once
before `hlt`, leaving the byte it read in RAX. The timer interrupt chose
which controller to acknowledge by comparing the interrupted program's RAX,
not the vector, against the local timer's vector 48. A `0` byte (0x30) plus
a PIT tick sent the acknowledgement to the local APIC; the PIC's in-service
bit for IRQ0 never cleared, and no timer or serial interrupt came again.
They reproduced it 3 of 3 on the U63 seed (512-byte bursts of `0` every
30 ms) and not on the fixed seed (2.9 MB over 180 s). So U63.log's
XSAVE/AVX guess was wrong.

They leave our **1 MB stall offsets** unexplained. Our side explains them:
the trigger needs an EMPTY ring, and both our feeders (cobblestone-qemu's
`ring_compile.py`, the transpiler's `guest.py`) preload the ring's first
megabyte and then refill it from the host. The guest can drain the ring
between refills, so an empty ring is likeliest at a 1 MB boundary: both
stalls, at 2,097,152 and 11,534,336, are exact multiples. Worth one line
back to Damian.

Nothing else touches our repos directly. The zig plug and every phase
signature the harnesses call are unchanged (`check-chapter`,
`lower-chapter`); the compiler changes are generic equality by dictionary
passing (Desugarer, TypeChecker, Lowering, X86_64).

## cobblestone-qemu: GREEN 5/5 in 9m26s, no change needed

    GREEN 5/5 in 9m26s    cobblestone-qemu cd868ab, sandbox 20260926T095758Z-u64
    fib 10s · zigemit 39s · codexir 3m32s · x86emit 4m18s (IR 1m30s, transpile 2m43s)
    verify   zig PASS · qemu PASS (IR identical to the native tool) · x86 PASS · boot PASS (6765)

U63's green run took 13m03s (codexir 5m12s, x86emit 6m09s), and this one ran
with the transpiler's three guests alongside it. No stall. One clean run
does not prove the fix -- the U63 stalls were intermittent -- but it is
consistent with it, and the guests' speed-up is too (below).

