## Rust on curated and safari: green on every arm

Rust is not parked as a verdict: it is our independent reading of what Codex
means, and it grades every Update. (Only new porting work was parked.)

rust-codex-compiler `7d1dd6b`: builtins at U64, +40, none removed (256-bit
vectors and masks, lane-wise vector equality, debug registers, arm64 timers,
`__heap-base`, U64's `__dderiv-Eq`). The probe had silently dropped
`identity-set-proc` from the types table, because U63 moved
`identity-admin-row` from TypeEnv.codex into Builtins.codex; it reads both
now, and names any declared type it cannot render. U63 also gave a vector
mask a lane type, which our `(vec-mask N)` has no slot for: the new
quantifier on `vec-select` and `mask-*` is bound and unused.

    ir-interp    cobblestone 28 agree (37s) · roc 46 agree (58s) · safari 54 agree (3m27s)
    ir-rust      cobblestone 28 agree · roc 46 agree · safari 54 agree
    ir-zig       cobblestone 28 match · roc 46 match · safari 54 match
    run-interp   cobblestone 28 match · roc 46 match · safari 54 match
    ir-diff      cobblestone 28 same · roc 44 same, 2 differ · safari 54 same
    cargo test   273 ok

ir-interp is the load-bearing one: codexrun interprets U64's own compiler
frontend (`~/codexir/codexir-subject.codex`) over each unit, and the IR it
produces must equal native codexir's on the same bytes. The two ir-diff
roc differences are U62's, at the same `MkTup2` token (COMPILER-85:
upstream instantiates constructor references, Rust does not).

